+# Privacy and third-party review
+
+Scope: the static Daily Grind website. This is an internal implementation inventory,
+not an independent audit, certification, or review of deployed infrastructure.
+
+## Data minimization and secure handling
+
+- No accounts, forms, payment collection, identifiers, cookies, browser storage,
+  analytics, tracking pixels, or client-side scripts.
+- Incoming requests necessarily involve an IP address, request path, and connection
+  metadata. The app processes these in memory and does not write access logs.
+- HTML and assets have Cache-Control: no-store. Browser history remains user-controlled.
+- No database or data export/delete endpoint is needed because no personal records
+  are stored. Reassess this before adding accounts, orders, rewards, or contact forms.
+- Explicit route allowlist keeps source, configuration, and arbitrary filesystem
+  files inaccessible. Errors do not expose request input or stack traces.
+- The CSP denies scripts, connections, frames, objects, forms, and unlisted resources.
+  Styles and images are same-origin. Referrer-Policy prevents referrer disclosure.
+- Runtime: built-in Node.js modules only. Keep Node on a supported, patched release.
+
+## Third-party inventory
+
+| Service | Current use | Visitor data sent by application | Review status |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash | Source of locally hosted coffee photograph | None; no runtime requests | Image delivery reviewed; licensing/attribution terms require operator review |
+| Analytics / ads / payment vendors | None | None | Not integrated |
+| Hosting / CDN / TLS provider | Selected by operator | May receive IP, paths, connection metadata | Pending deployment-specific assessment |
+
+Photo source: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085
+
+## Required deployment review
+
+1. Enable HTTPS and redirect HTTP at the TLS terminator. Set NODE_ENV=production
+   when starting this server behind HTTPS to emit HSTS. Do not expose its HTTP
+   listener directly in production. Confirm the proxy preserves all response headers.
+2. Inventory hosting, CDN, logging, error monitoring, and backup vendors. Identify
+   exactly what each collects, why it is needed, where it is processed, retention,
+   access restrictions, deletion mechanisms, and applicable contractual terms.
+3. Disable unneeded provider access logs. Where needed, minimize logged fields,
+   exclude query strings and payloads, restrict access, and set a documented short
+   retention period. Never log secrets or personal data. Review the user-facing
+   policy against actual infrastructure settings.
+4. Arrange an independent third-party review before introducing personal-data
+   processing. Record reviewer, scope, date, evidence, findings, and remediation.
+   Repeat after material changes and at least annually for integrated vendors.
+5. Keep audit records private when they contain sensitive infrastructure details.
+   Publish only verified conclusions and an accurate audit status.
+6. For future data collection, document purpose, minimum required fields, retention
+   and deletion jobs, user rights, transport and storage protection, authentication,
+   least-privilege access, incident handling, and vendor review before release.
+
+## Validation and header references
+
+Run `npm test` for HTTP/header, method, route, and static asset policy checks.
+No manual browser verification is required.
+
+- https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy
+- https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Referrer-Policy
